---
import { nav, site } from '../data/site';
---
<footer class="foot" data-tone="void">
  <div class="foot__grid">
    <p class="foot__about">
      {site.name} is an unofficial explainer of <cite>{site.film.name}</cite> ({site.film.year}), directed by {site.film.director}.
      It is not affiliated with the film or its studios. Line drawings and the Endurance ring are drawn in code for this site.
    </p>
    <nav class="foot__nav" aria-label="Footer">
      <ul>
        {nav.map((n) => (
          <li><a class="dilate draw" data-text={n.label} href={n.href}><span class="dilate__t">{n.label}</span></a></li>
        ))}
        <li><a class="dilate draw" data-text="Back to the top" href="#main"><span class="dilate__t">Back to the top</span></a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <p class="foot__colophon">Set in Archivo and IBM Plex Mono. Numbers marked ≈ are estimates; the film states the rest.</p>
    <p class="foot__colophon">Photographs: NASA/JPL-Caltech/Space Science Institute (Cassini), NASA Goddard (black hole simulations by Jeremy Schnittman and Brian P. Powell; Antarctica; the Deep Star Maps of the Scientific Visualization Studio), NASA Ames Research Center (space colony study, 1976), NASA and ESA Hubble (a dwarf galaxy, the Ultraviolet Ultra Deep Field and the lensing cluster SDSS J1038+4849), NASA ISS Expedition 40, USDA. All public domain, toned for this site. The wormhole flight is ray traced for this site through the metric in “Visualizing Interstellar’s Wormhole” (James, von Tunzelmann, Franklin and Thorne, 2015) over those NASA images. Film stills of Cooper and Murph, the Ranger, TARS, Gargantua and the watch, the production still of the tesseract set, and DNEG’s wormhole render are © Warner Bros. Entertainment and Paramount Pictures, shown for commentary. The score is synthesized in your browser for this site; it is not the film’s.</p>
  </div>
</footer>
